[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Creating a node still costs a reload each, where every other write batches

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[WORK]
UID: WORK-SCRIBE-NEW-DOES-NOT-BATCH
TITLE: Creating a node still costs a reload each, where every other write batches
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Every other write defers its reload, so a run of them pays one. `new` does
not: it reloads inside the operation, because the @repo grammar alias is
resolved by the index builder and not by the reader, so a document assembled
purely in memory has no grammar. Measured: three news cost 2462 ms, about
820 ms each, against 17 ms for three sets.

The shape that would fix it is splitting the operation. Write the skeleton,
mark the workspace dirty, and add the node after the deferred reload rather
than before it -- so N new nodes cost N cheap writes and one reload. A
relation naming a node created earlier in the same batch would not resolve
until that reload, which the workspace's retry path already handles by
re-deriving once.

The alternative is adopting a document in memory: build its DocumentMeta and
insert it into the document tree by hand. That removes the reload entirely
and is how the containers would have to be touched directly, which the
workspace deliberately does not do today.

Filed rather than guessed at. Until it lands, `new` is the one command still
paying a load per call.
<<<
